    text: "Money and currency are fundamental to daily life and international communication. The major English-speaking currencies include the British pound sterling (£, GBP), the US dollar ($, USD), the Australian dollar (AUD), and the Canadian dollar (CAD). Each currency has coins and banknotes (bills in American English). British coins include the penny (1p), five pence (5p), ten pence (10p), twenty pence (20p), fifty pence (50p), one pound (£1), and two pounds (£2). British banknotes come in £5, £10, £20, and £50 denominations. American coins include the penny (1¢), nickel (5¢), dime (10¢), and quarter (25¢), while bills come in $1, $5, $10, $20, $50, and $100. Talking about money involves prices, payments, and transactions. You can pay in cash, by card (debit or credit), by bank transfer, or using contactless payment. A receipt proves your purchase, change is the money returned when you overpay, and a tip is extra money given for good service. Understanding exchange rates is essential for travel: 'How much is a pound worth in dollars?' Budget vocabulary — income, expenses, savings, debt, interest, tax, and inflation — helps discuss personal and national finances.",
